  "attributionNote": "Revealed by Bahá’u’lláh in Baghdad in Persian, and translated by Shoghi Effendi; the Reference Library publishes it in the collection The Call of the Divine Beloved.",
  "description": "The Four Valleys describes the ways by which the seeker reaches the goal: through the heart and the loved one, through the spirit and the beauty of God, through the mind and the infinite, and through what the treatise leaves unnamed. It is addressed to a Sufi teacher, draws its argument from the Qur’an and the Persian poets, and stands as the companion to the Seven Valleys.",
  "historicalContext": "Revealed in the same Baghdad period as the Seven Valleys and addressed to a Sufi leader, it is read with it as the second of the two mystical treatises of the Bahá’í writings, describing the same path by another set of stages.",
